course: books
lesson: 77
title: Just So Stories (Rudyard Kipling), 4: How the Leopard got his Spots, part 1 (the game goes into the forest)
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: Long ago the Leopard lived on the bare, hot, sandy High Veldt, and he was exactly the same sandy-yellow-brown as the ground, so he could lie by a stone and jump out on the Giraffe, the Zebra and the antelopes before they saw him.
explain: A man with a bow and arrows hunted there with him, until the hunted animals had had enough: led by the long-legged Giraffe, they ran away to a great forest full of stripy, speckly shadows.
explain: After a long time standing half in shade and half in sun, the Giraffe grew blotchy, the Zebra grew stripy, and the antelopes grew darker, so you could hear them and smell them but hardly ever see them.
explain: Back on the empty veldt the hungry Leopard and the hunter eat rats and beetles, get a terrible tummy-ache, and ask the wise Baboon, Baviaan, where all the animals have gone; he only winks and gives them riddles about "spots" and "change".
explain: They find the shadowy forest and can smell and hear Giraffe and Zebra but cannot see them at all, and the part ends with them puzzled in the speckled dark.
fact: The story says this happened "In the days when everybody started fair", on the "High Veldt": a real high, open grassland in South Africa (today spelled Highveld; "veld" means open country).
fact: Kipling keeps saying "'sclusively", his baby-talk for "exclusively", which here means "only, completely".
fact: The Giraffe, the Zebra, the Eland, the Koodoo (kudu) and the Hartebeest lived there and were all "sandy-yellow-brownish all over"; eland, kudu and hartebeest are real African antelopes.
fact: The Leopard "matched the 'sclusively yellowish-greyish-brownish colour of the High Veldt to one hair", and would "surprise them out of their jumpsome lives".
fact: The Leopard hunted with a man who had bows and arrows; parts of this story use old words and ideas about people that are racist and wrong, so we do not repeat them, and in these lessons we simply call him "the hunter".
fact: "Bit by bit--the Giraffe began it, because his legs were the longest--they went away from the High Veldt" to a forest full of "stripy, speckly, patchy-blatchy shadows".
fact: There "the Giraffe grew blotchy, and the Zebra grew stripy, and the Eland and the Koodoo grew darker, with little wavy grey lines on their backs like bark on a tree trunk".
fact: The Leopard and the hunter grew so hungry that they "ate rats and beetles and rock-rabbits" and "had the Big Tummy-ache, both together".
fact: "Baviaan" (an Afrikaans word for baboon) is "the dog-headed, barking Baboon, who is Quite the Wisest Animal in All South Africa"; real baboons do have long dog-like snouts and give loud barks.
fact: The hunter asks "the present habitat of the aboriginal Fauna", which means "where the wild animals of this land live now" ("aboriginal" here = there from the beginning, "fauna" = animals, "flora" = plants); the book says he always used long words because he was a grown-up.
fact: Baviaan's riddles: "The game has gone into other spots; and my advice to you, Leopard, is to go into other spots", and "The aboriginal Fauna has joined the aboriginal Flora because it was high time for a change" ("game" = wild animals hunted for food).
fact: The forest is "speckled and sprottled and spottled, dotted and splashed and slashed and hatched and cross-hatched with shadows"; Kipling says "Say that quickly aloud".
fact: The Leopard remembers that "Giraffe is about seventeen feet high" and Zebra "about four and a half feet high" (he knows the Zebra's size well, because he used to hunt him and crunch his marrow-bones); "Fiddle!" is an old word for "Nonsense!".
fact: The hunter says the animals should "show up in this dark place like ripe bananas in a smokehouse", that is, bright yellow things in a dark room.
fact: Real biology: animals do not change colour by standing in shadows; their patterns are passed down from parents, and over a very long time the ones whose colours hid them best survived more often and had more young.
fact: Real biology: the "rock-rabbit" is the rock hyrax, a small furry animal of African rocks which, oddly, is a distant relative of the elephant; the Quagga, a half-striped zebra named in the story, died out in 1883.
exercise: Where did the Leopard live at the start? | On the High Veldt | In a shadowy forest | In a Howling Desert | On an island in the Red Sea
exercise: What is the High Veldt? | A high, open grassland in South Africa | A thick, dark forest full of tall trees | A salty lake in the middle of a desert | A rocky island near the coast of Arabia
exercise: What colour was the Leopard then? | Sandy-yellowish-brownish, like the veldt | Black with little five-finger spots | Grey with long black and purple stripes | Golden-yellow with big chestnut blotches
exercise: Why was the Leopard's colour "very bad" for the Giraffe and the Zebra? | He matched the ground, so they could not see him coming | He was so bright that he frightened them every day | He looked like a Giraffe, so they let him come near | He was so pale that he scared away all their food
exercise: What did "'sclusively" mean in this story? | Only, completely | Sadly, slowly | Never, nowhere | Nearly, but not quite
exercise: Who hunted with the Leopard on the High Veldt? | A man with bows and arrows | The Baboon called Baviaan | A Djinn in a cloud of dust | The Parsee with his knife
exercise: Which animal began the escape from the High Veldt? | The Giraffe, because his legs were the longest | The Zebra, because he could run the fastest | The Eland, because he was the most frightened | The Quagga, because he knew the forest best
exercise: Where did the animals go? | A great forest full of stripy, speckly shadows | A desert where nothing ever grew but thorns | An island far away in the middle of the sea | The banks of a wide river full of crocodiles
exercise: In the story, how did the Giraffe get his blotches? | By standing half in the shade and half out of it | By rolling in the mud of a river every morning | By a Djinn who was angry about his long legs | By a man pressing his fingers all over him
exercise: What grew on the backs of the Eland and the Koodoo? | Little wavy grey lines like bark on a tree trunk | Big round spots like the Leopard has today | Long woolly hair to keep off the forest flies | Small humps to store fat for the long journey
exercise: In the forest, which TWO senses could still find the animals? | Smell and hearing | Seeing and hearing | Seeing and tasting | Tasting and touching
exercise: What did the Leopard and the hunter eat when they were starving? | Rats and beetles and rock-rabbits | A big cake baked on an oil-stove | Melons and sugar-cane and bananas | Every fish they could find in the sea
exercise: What happened after they ate it? | They had the Big Tummy-ache, both together | They fell fast asleep in the hot sun at once | They felt strong and ran into the forest | They grew spots and stripes on their skin
exercise: Who is "Quite the Wisest Animal in All South Africa"? | Baviaan the Baboon | The Leopard | The Giraffe | The tall Ostrich aunt
exercise: What did Baviaan do when they first asked him? | Winked, because he knew | Laughed and ran away | Told them straight away | Said "Humph!" and left
exercise: What does "game" mean when the Leopard asks "Where has all the game gone?" | Wild animals hunted for food | Toys for children to play with | A football match on the veldt | A board game like chess
exercise: What does "aboriginal Fauna" mean in the hunter's question? | The wild animals that belong to that land | The tall trees that grow in a dark forest | The sandy rocks of the High Veldt grassland | The people who used to live in the desert
exercise: Why did the hunter use long words like "habitat" and "Fauna"? | He was a grown-up, and grown-ups use long words | He wanted to trick Baviaan into telling the truth | He was trying to frighten the Leopard by talking | He could not remember the short words for them
exercise: What advice did Baviaan give the Leopard? | To go into other spots as soon as he could | To eat more rats and beetles for his tea every day | To go home and wait on the High Veldt | To climb up a tree and stay there all day
exercise: What does "Fiddle!" mean when the Leopard says it? | Nonsense! | Play me some music! | Hurry up, you! | Well done, you!
exercise: How tall does the Leopard remember the Giraffe to be? | About seventeen feet | About four and a half feet | About two and a half feet | About seventy-five feet
exercise: What did the hunter say Giraffe and Zebra should look like in the dark forest? | Ripe bananas in a smokehouse | A sun-flower against a fence | A heap of pebbles on the ground | A bar of chocolate in the snow
exercise: Real biology: how do animals really get their patterns? | They are passed down from their parents | By standing in the shade for a long time | By rubbing against trees in the forest | By eating spotted and striped plants
exercise: Real biology: what is a "rock-rabbit"? | A rock hyrax, a distant cousin of the elephant | A small rabbit that lives inside hard rocks | A kind of lizard that looks a bit like a rabbit | A baby baboon that sits on top of the rocks
exercise: Thinking back: in the Rhinoceros story, where did the Parsee live? | On an uninhabited island by the Red Sea | On the High Veldt of South Africa | In a great forest full of shadows | In the middle of a Howling Desert
exercise: Thinking back: on which day of the week did the Camel stop working? | Monday | Thursday | Saturday | Wednesday
test: Noah reckons: "The animals went to the forest cos they liked the shade." Put him right. | They were escaping from the Leopard and the hunter | They had eaten up every bit of grass on the High Veldt | Baviaan told them to go there and change colour | The Djinn of All Deserts sent them away to work
test: Mia, Gen Z: "Leopard on the veldt was highkey invisible, it's giving ninja." ("highkey" = very obviously, "it's giving" = it looks like.) Why was he so hard to see there? | His colour matched the sand, rocks and grass exactly | He only ever came out to hunt in the middle of night | He hid behind Baviaan, who was much bigger than him | He could make himself small like a rock-rabbit at will
test: What would have happened if the Giraffe and the Zebra had stayed on the bare High Veldt? | They would have stayed easy to find and catch | They would have turned spotty and stripy anyway | The Leopard would have gone to the forest alone | Baviaan would have hidden them under his arms
test: True or false: the Leopard and the hunter could not find the animals in the forest because they had forgotten their smell. | False: they could smell and hear them, but not see them | True: they had been away so long they forgot their smell | True: the forest smelt too strongly of the dark trees | False: they could see them clearly but could not catch them
test: Baviaan says "go into other spots". What is the double meaning? | Other places, and spots on your skin | Other animals, and little spots of rain | Other forests, and spots of mud | Other games, and spots on dice
test: Grandad, Northern Ireland: "Yon baboon's a cute one, so he is, winkin' away." ("yon" = that, "cute" here = sly, clever.) What did Baviaan's wink show? | He knew the answer but would not say it plainly | He had something in his eye from the dusty veldt | He was making fun of the Leopard's sandy colour | He was falling asleep in the very hot afternoon
test: Freddie says: "Giraffes got patches from standing under trees, my book says so." Put him right. | That is only Kipling's story; real patterns come from parents | Yes, all giraffes are born plain and turn patchy in the shade | Giraffes get their patches from the mud they roll in | Giraffes paint patches on each other with their tongues
test: In the Camel story the animals held "a palaver" about the idle Camel. In this story, who is asked for advice? | Baviaan the Baboon | The Djinn of All Deserts | The Man | The 'Stute Fish
test: Lola, Australia: "The Leopard and that bloke were starvin', reckon they'd eat a servo pie." ("servo" = petrol station.) What did they really eat? | Rats, beetles and rock-rabbits | A cake as big as a table | Every fish in the whole sea | Thorns and milkweed
test: In the Camel story the animals had to change because they were lazy or greedy. In this story, why do the Giraffe and Zebra change? | To hide from the animals that hunted them | Because they were idle and would not work | Because they stole a cake from a Parsee | Because they asked too many questions
test: Which earlier helper in the book is most like Baviaan, a clever creature who gives a tricky answer? | The 'Stute Fish, who sent the Whale to find a man | The Ox, who came with the yoke on his neck to plough | The Rhino, who came down to the beach with few manners | The Horse, who went and told the Man about the Camel
test: Kayden, Gen Alpha: "Leopard said he remembers the zebra's height cos of the marrow-bones, that's sus." ("sus" = suspicious, odd.) Why does he remember them so well? | He used to hunt and eat them | They were his best friends | He measured them for fun | Baviaan had told him all
test: Old Texas rancher: "Them critters skedaddled to the woods, and that big cat was plumb out of luck." ("skedaddled" = ran off, "plumb" = totally.) Which animal led them? | The Giraffe | The striped Zebra | The big Eland | The Koodoo with wavy lines
test: ★★★ The Leopard says Giraffe is "about seventeen feet high" and Zebra "about four and a half feet high". How much taller is Giraffe? | Twelve and a half feet | Thirteen and a half feet | Eleven and a half feet | Twenty-one and a half feet
test: ★★★ Trap: the story says the animals could "very seldom" be seen in the forest. Which is true? | They could be seen sometimes, if you knew precisely where to look | They could never be seen at all, not even by daylight | They could be seen easily, but only at night | They could only be seen by Baviaan the Baboon
test: ★★★ "Say that quickly aloud": "speckled and sprottled and spottled, dotted and splashed and slashed and hatched and cross-hatched". How many of these shadow words are there? | Eight | Seven | Nine | Six
exam: Identify the animal which, according to the narrative, initiated the departure from the High Veldt, together with the reason stated. | The Giraffe, his legs being the longest | The Zebra, his speed being the greatest | The Leopard, his hunger being the sharpest | The Eland, his fear being the strongest
exam: Ascertain the meaning of the expression "the aboriginal Fauna" as employed by the hunter in his enquiry. | The animals native to that region | The flowering plants native to that region | The people formerly native to that region | The rocks and minerals native to that region
exam: Select the response that most accurately characterises the condition of the Leopard and the hunter subsequent to their diet of rats, beetles and rock-rabbits. | They suffered the Big Tummy-ache together | They acquired spots and stripes forthwith | They were restored to vigour and strength | They were rendered entirely invisible
exam: State, in accordance with established biology, the manner in which the patterns of animals' coats are acquired. | By inheritance from the parents, shaped over many generations | By prolonged standing in the shadows cast by forest trees | By the deliberate painting of the coat with mud and charcoal | By the consumption of plants bearing spotted or striped leaves
